- QUALITY IS ONE DECISION NOW, MADE BEFORE THE TAKE, AND IT BINDS BOTH HALVES (UI1, 2026-08-30,
  Robert's eight-item UI pass: "make chatgpt/claudecode effort-like slider for our quality choosing …
  make it not possible to choose higher quality that was choosen before start of record to save
  resources on other processes"). It used to be TWO decisions that could not see each other — capture
  ran at whatever the source offered and the export ladder was picked afterwards — so the expensive
  half, the encoders running while the take is live, was never told what the user wanted.
  `core/qualityStep.ts` is the single ceiling both halves read: 540p · 720p · 1080p · 1440p · max,
  the export ladder's own rungs plus F18's source step, tied to QUALITY_TIERS by test. It DRIVES the
  flags F15/F18 already built rather than adding machinery — `captureCeilingLongEdge`,
  `sourceResEnabled`, `captureRateCeiling`, `tiersForTake` — and `max` is what turns source
  resolution and 60 fps on (Robert: "max - maximum resolution, 60 fps, all maximum"). THE TAKE
  CARRIES ITS OWN CEILING (`Recording.qualityStep`), so a take opened from the takes list is capped
  by what was chosen for IT; a take from before UI1 has none and is uncapped, which is the ladder it
  was made under. DEFAULT IS 1080p AND MUST STAY THERE: the composite is written at a 1920 long edge
  and the untouched export packet-copies it, so a higher default would silently turn every export
  into a full re-render — the frozen "instant default export" rule, broken by arithmetic. Pinned by
  test. COST STATED: a user who never touches the slider now captures at a 1920 long edge instead of
  up to 2560, and can no longer export 1440p from such a take. That is the resource saving Robert
  asked for and the slider is the first thing above the chips.
- THE EXPORT IS A BACKGROUND JOB IN A DOCK ON EVERY SCREEN (2026-08-30, third answer in one day to
  where the export lives; supersedes the strips-in-the-slider-slot pass, which had itself replaced
  the two-panel screen — `AppMode` lost 'share' then, and lost 'exporting' now).
  WHAT FORCED IT, and it is a bug worth remembering: Robert exported a big take and saw "99 %
  Finalizing…" THE MOMENT he pressed Export, sat behind that number for five minutes, pressed
  Cancel, and got the download. F16's join was lying three ways at once — choose.ts reported a
  HARD-CODED `finalizing 0.99` and then silently awaited the whole in-flight pre-render; the
  claimer's abort was wired to NOTHING (the pre-render owns its own controller, and the export's
  signal was never connected to it); and onExport never re-checked its signal after the await, so
  the cancel's only visible effect was whatever the render did when it finished anyway. Fixed at the
  joint: takePrerender now hands over the job's real progress AND its abort controller, choose.ts
  forwards both, the settle handler keeps a claimed file's bytes (it used to drop them the moment
  the job was retired), and a cancelled job DELIVERS NOTHING — liveness is re-checked after the
  export settles, whatever settled it. Pinned by tests in prerender.test.ts and exportJobs.test.ts.
  THE REST OF HIS MESSAGE IS THE FEATURE: "rendering process shown in block like on screenshot in
  bottom of screen layout and happening further if i switch app screen, independetly, and i want it
  sirvive refresh and continue, and several rendering at the same time possible too."
  compose/exportJobs.ts owns it: pressing Export (or For AI — same lane) creates a PERSISTED job
  (jobsRepo) and returns immediately; the editor never locks, because the job
  snapshotted the edit at the press. The dock (ExportDock, fixed bottom-centre, rendered by App on
  every screen) shows one strip per job — real %, phase, measured ETA, a Cancel that reaches the
  worker — and a finished job becomes the old saved strip (Save again · cloud link · dismiss).
  The download still fires the moment the job finishes; the job then copies the file to its own
  OPFS key (`xjob-<id>`) AFTER the download starts, so instant stays instant and Save again / the
  cloud button survive a refresh. REFRESH RESTARTS a running job from zero — a muxer's half-written
  file is not a file, so restarting is the honest "continue" — at most 3 attempts before the row
  says it stopped, and done/failed rows expire after 24 h (the file is in Downloads; the row is a
  convenience, not an archive). Duplicate press of the same output JOINS the running job (same
  argument as F16, one level up); a different tier is a second, genuinely parallel job.
  THE JOBS TABLE IS ITS OWN DATABASE (`inout-jobs`), AND THE `inout` VERSION CAN NEVER GO UP
  AGAIN — the first cut bumped it 2→3 and WEDGED PROD WITHIN THE HOUR, found by driving it: an
  IndexedDB version bump needs a versionchange that ANY old-version connection blocks forever (an
  old build's tab, a bfcached page — nothing shipped ever registered onversionchange), and every
  open issued after the blocked upgrade queues behind it, so boot recovery hung silently and
  STOP() HUNG SAVING THE RECORDING — a lost take, on every profile with an old tab anywhere. A
  brand-new database has no old holders on any profile, so it opens instantly, everywhere.
  Connections now close themselves on versionchange (hygiene shipped for the future), but tabs
  already in the field never will; the rule is pinned as a comment on DB_VERSION.
  PARALLEL EXPORTS MADE THE SCRATCH SWEEP A WEAPON: each export worker carries its own scratch.ts
  instance, so worker B's start-of-export sweep read worker A's half-written scratch as a leftover
  and would have deleted it mid-write. Scratch keys carry their birth time now and the sweep skips
  anything younger than 6 h (pre-stamp keys parse as ancient, which is correct — they are from
  builds that no longer run). ExportResult carries `scratchKey` so the paths that finished with the
  bytes free the file precisely instead of waiting for age. Cost stated: the app shell grew
  275.6 → 281.5 kB (+1.8 kB gzip — the dock UI itself); the compose graph still loads only on an
  export press, or at boot when persisted jobs exist.
- I INVENTED A PROGRESS INDICATOR TWICE AND HAD TO BE SENT A SCREENSHOT (UI1, 2026-08-30, three
  rounds: "slider dont look like claude code effort at all, fix it", then "slider dont fucking look
  like this, is in it?" with a picture of the real control). The class of mistake is worth naming
  because both attempts made it in the same direction — I kept reaching for a FILL:
    attempt 1 — thin rail, continuous accent fill, round handle. Reads as "43 % of the way through
                something", which is the one thing a stepped control must never look like.
    attempt 2 — the same fill cut into blocks. A five-bar signal meter: "more is better" rather than
                "you are on step three of five".
  THE REAL ONE HAS NO FILL ANYWHERE. One continuous dark track, a faint dot at each stop inside it,
  and a white rounded-RECTANGLE handle sitting on the stop you are on. The only thing that moves is
  the handle, and nothing is coloured, because nothing is being measured — a step is a choice, not an
  amount. The handle travels INSET by half its own width so it never hangs off either end, and the
  dots share that inset so it always lands exactly on one; the position is ONE expression used by the
  dots, the handle and the labels (`--qs-tw` on the .qs root, not on the track — declared on the
  track it was invisible to the labels, which are its sibling, and the three middle steps collapsed
  into a heap at the left edge).
  Export and For AI sit on the track's own line ("export buttons on same line with slider right to it
  i said"). BOTH copies are captionless now — the editor's ("no need for top and bottom captions")
  and the capture screen's ("remove all three captions near slider in main screen"). The step names
  under the track say which one is chosen; the sentence each step carried survives as the track's
  tooltip, and the instant-vs-re-render difference as the Export button's, because "copies in a
  second" vs "re-renders for minutes" is a real difference between two presses of the same button.
- THE EDITOR OPENED INSIDE A 4 ms HOLE, AND THAT IS WHY THE CAMERA COULD NOT BE DRAGGED (fixed
  2026-08-30; Robert: "draging camera dont work … it was working before, i m almost sure" — he was
  right to be unsure, and I was wrong to answer "it never worked there"). It worked the moment the
  playhead moved off zero, and on any take whose video happened to start first.
  THE CHAIN: devices do not arm on the same millisecond, and a take's zero is the earliest channel of
  ANY kind — normally the microphone. So the video channels typically begin a few ms later (measured
  on a take here: mic at 0, camera and screen both at 4). For those 4 ms the take genuinely IS
  audio-only and `activeChannelsAt` reports it correctly. The editor parks its playhead at 0, so it
  opened in that hole: no video element drawn, a black stage — and because `.is-hidden` is
  `visibility: hidden`, WHICH REMOVES AN ELEMENT FROM HIT TESTING, a camera PiP that could not be
  grabbed at all. The drag code was never involved. Found with `document.elementFromPoint`, which
  returned the PiP's PARENT at the PiP's own centre; the black opening frame had been visible in my
  own screenshots for several rounds and I had read it as "the take starts on a black frame".
  THE FIX IS PRESENTATION ONLY: Player draws the first 150 ms as the composition the take OPENS with
  rather than as the arming noise it is. Elements are still seeked to the real instant (a video
  seeked before its own start clamps to its first frame), so it changes which surfaces are visible,
  not what they show, and nothing past the head window moves — a genuine mid-take camera gap still
  hides the camera. `compose` calls `activeChannelsAt` itself and is untouched: the exported file
  keeps exactly the frames it always had.
  THE LESSON: `visibility: hidden` is not a paint state, it is a HIT-TESTING state. Anything
  interactive that can be hidden this way stops being reachable, and the symptom is reported as
  "the control is broken" rather than "the control is invisible".

- THE CAMERA CAN BE MOVED WHILE THE TAKE RUNS (UI1, Robert: "draggin camera small view stopped
  working while recording"). It had never worked there — the editor's PiP is a keyframe track over a
  FINISHED take, and the capture preview only ever drew the default corner — so nothing regressed;
  the feature was missing. It belongs here more than in the editor, because the thing you are trying
  not to cover is on screen WHILE you record.
  FOUR LAYERS, capability-gated so a take nobody drags is byte-identical to one made before this:
  the compositor takes a `campose` message and `pipRect` reads it, falling through to the constants
  when it is null · liveCompositeV2 exposes `setCameraPose`, and v1 DECLARES it optional and does not
  implement it (v1 composites on the main thread from the UI's own <video> elements, so a live pose
  there is the editor's job) · the session forwards it and stamps the final pose on the Recording ·
  `defaultEditState` seeds the editor's camera track from that pose.
  THE LAST ONE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS FOUR LAYERS: the composite is written
  with the moved PiP and the default export packet-copies that file, so an editor that opened on the
  default corner would be previewing a frame the export cannot produce. The pose goes through
  `poseToRect` in all three renderers — capture preview, compositor, editor — so it is one piece of
  arithmetic and not three implementations of a corner. Verified on prod: dragged to (0.258, 0.296)
  mid-take, the stored `Recording.cameraPose` reads that, and the editor's PiP opens at (0.259,
  0.297).
- THE RECORDING PREVIEW FILLS WHAT IS THERE (UI1, Robert: "make screen fill so no gap here"). It was
  absolutely positioned over the whole screen and sized from `100dvh - 300px`, a guess at the
  controls' height made when the control bar was taller; on a recording screen (chips + record
  button, no slider) the guess was ~150 px too big and left a black band exactly that tall. It is a
  flex child of the body now and takes its bound from the box it is actually in (`container-type:
  size` + `100cqh`), the same fix the editor's stage got, for the same reason: an estimate of
  available height is a bug waiting for the layout below it to change.

- THE TIMELINE'S AXIS IS PIECEWISE, AND A CLOSED CUT TAKES NO WIDTH (2026-08-30, Robert: "add delete
  button, which will remove it completle and other part will slide to each other smoothly"). `x()` is
  linear in recording time everywhere except across a hole the user has CLOSED, which contributes
  nothing; `msAtClient` inverts it, so a click still lands on the instant under it. Every position on
  the timeline goes through those two, which is what makes the collapse one change instead of one per
  element.
  THE COST THAT COULD NOT BE SKIPPED, and it is why this was never a button: a lane was ONE element
  spanning the channel with its filmstrip stretched across it, which is only correct while the axis
  is linear. A lane is drawn in PIECES now — one per stretch still shown — and each piece takes a
  SLICE of the strip, scaled by how much of the channel it covers and offset to where that slice
  begins. With nothing closed that is exactly one piece spanning the whole channel, i.e. the bar it
  always was. The trim edges moved OUT of the bar to the lane track: they are one instant each and a
  closed hole must not give them two homes. `x(duration)` is no longer a width anywhere — `spanW(a,b)`
  is, and every width was converted.
  CLOSING IS A VIEW STATE and deliberately not on `EditState`: the material was already excluded when
  the cut was made, and this only decides whether the timeline still draws the hole it left.
  `EditState` is a contract the export reads. Keyed by the hole's own start instant, so adding a cut
  elsewhere cannot collapse the wrong one. The slide is animated for 240 ms and ONLY then — the same
  transition left on permanently would put lag behind every trim drag.
  Verified on prod: split, drag left to cut 5 s, close it — the clips slide together, the ruler skips
  the closed stretch, the filmstrip is continuous across the seam, and a global trim afterwards still
  reads 0:25 -> 0:20 with 8 lane pieces (4 channels x 2).
- EVERY EXCLUDED STRETCH IS ONE THING, AND YOU CAN SEE THROUGH IT (2026-08-30, Robert: "cutted zone
  keep slightly transparent to see whats cut, for cutted zone from beggining and end apply same
  shit"). The zone was an OPAQUE scrim — honest that the material is gone, useless for judging
  whether the right thing was cut, because you could not see what you had thrown away. It is 55 %
  now, under a hatch. And the take's HEAD AND TAIL are cuts too — the global trim excludes them
  exactly as a mid-cut excludes what sits between two clips — so they stopped being a plain dim band
  and became the same zone, with the same undo and the same close, differing only in which edit field
  the undo resets. One renderer for all three (`zoneActions`), so they cannot drift.
  CLOSING IS NOT A ONE-WAY DOOR: a closed stretch leaves a seam and the seam is a BUTTON that opens
  it again. Shipping the collapse without that was my gap, not a request.
  The two buttons are stacked, not side by side ("i said delete UNDER cancel"), which also means they
  survive in a much narrower cut — the threshold fell from 44 px to 24.
- THE PLAYHEAD CRAWLED WHILE YOU DRAGGED A CUT (2026-08-30, Robert: "split grabber moving that shit
  on timeline that 'now' line"). It is stored as an OUTPUT time and DRAWN at the recording instant
  that output time maps to — and cutting is precisely the operation that changes that mapping. So
  every pixel of a cut drag re-pointed an unchanged output time at a different recording instant, and
  the line walked across the timeline while the picture under it never moved. The instant is captured
  at gesture start and re-derived into output time on every move (`holdPlayhead`), which is the trick
  the speed control already used for the same reason; applied to a cut edge, a touching boundary and
  the global trim, the three gestures that move the mapping. Verified on the local mirror: playhead
  pinned at x=688 through a drag that opened a 2 s cut to its left, its output time correctly
  re-derived 0:05 -> 0:03.
- A REACT KEY WAS KILLING THE DRAG, AND THE ASYMMETRY WAS THE CLUE (2026-08-30, Robert: "still cant
  drag right grabber after split, barely moves"). The segment wrapper was keyed `seg-${sg.startMs}`.
  Dragging a boundary RIGHT moves the NEXT clip's `startMs` — which WAS that key — so React unmounted
  the subtree holding the very handle under the pointer, taking its listeners and its pointer capture
  with it: the gesture died after one move, which is exactly what "barely moves" describes. Dragging
  LEFT moves the previous clip's `endMs`, which appears in no key, so that direction always worked.
  Keyed by POSITION now, never by a value the gesture itself mutates — the same trap was in the zone
  and seam keys and was fixed there too.
  THE LESSON, and it is general: a React key built from mutable data is a self-destruct on any
  element that edits that data. It presents as "the control is broken", not as "the component
  remounted", and it fails in ONE DIRECTION ONLY, which is the fingerprint to look for.
- THE RULER SITS ABOVE THE LANES (2026-08-30, Robert: "make timeline part with time that under input,
  make it above it"). The overlay starts below it, so every child keeps its offsets relative to the
  lanes rather than each one being nudged; the playhead reaches UP into it (`top: -26px`) so the time
  under the head stays readable.
- A CUT BOUNDARY IS ONE HANDLE, NOT TWO (2026-08-30, Robert: "grabbers to ugly and hard to distinct"
  + "when i split record i cant drag left part but right part grabber dont moves" — one defect, two
  reports). A fresh split leaves the end of one clip and the start of the next on the SAME pixel, and
  each of those edges can only travel one way: the left clip's end is clamped by the next clip's
  start, the right clip's start by the previous clip's end. So half of every grab was a no-op, on a
  7 px target, with nothing on screen saying which half was held. Splitting the hit area (the first
  fix) made both halves reachable and left each one-directional, which is the half he reported.
  Now the two halves share ONE gesture: the boundary stays where it was pressed and dragging opens
  the cut on whichever side it is dragged towards, so it is reversible — drag back through and that
  side closes while the other opens.
  THE GRIP TOOK THREE ATTEMPTS and the first two failed the same way. v1 was a 2 px accent line — a
  MARK: it says where the cut is and nothing about being draggable. v2 was a 13x30 tab with two
  ridges floating mid-lane — an object, but an OPAQUE one pasted over the frames ("grabbers ugly as
  fuck"). The lanes are a dense picture, so a handle here has to be present without covering them:
