THE COLD GATE WAS MEASURING PAST THE END OF ITS OWN RULER, AND IT IS FIXED (GATE-alias, 2026-08-25).
`--cold=3` used to fail ~20 % of runs with a whole file scored a few hundred ms out. The cause was not
the product and not, as first written here, an ambiguous pairing: a cold AudioContext's STARTUP STALL
reached 537 ms (beep arrivals 1537/2537/3537/4537/5537 — residual 537 on every one, while the flash
side stalled 20), and 537 is past two hard limits at once. The skew estimator refused anything over
450 ms, so there was no correction and the gate fell to its RAW rung; and the click↔flash window is
half the 1000 ms grid wide and centred on ZERO, so the +562 ms offset that stall produces is not
representable and came back as −438. Four fixes: the stall is now ARITHMETIC (a beep can only render
late, so arrival = k·interval + stall and k = floor(arrival/interval) — no sweep, no clamp, missed
first beep still fine); the pairing is TOLD where to look (the rig measures both reference schedules
before it decodes, and their difference is the expected offset); the pairing picks by CONSENSUS rather
than by pair count (injection-proven: the old rule reported −400 where the truth was +50); and THE RIG
WARMS ITS ENCODER as production does at mount, because a fresh Chrome process's first VideoEncoder ate
the opening seconds of every 6 s take — which is why the packet-copy exports sometimes contained no
flashes at all and their sync silently went unmeasured. Two vacuity holes closed with it: a fast path
that RAN but produced no number now fails instead of passing. AFTER: 22 cold runs, zero sync failures,
zero aliasing, every run on the `metric=symmetric` rung; sync 36.6-65.4 ms, instant 41.8-78.5.
The per-run line now prints `metric=` and `skew=<audio>/<flash>`, and a failing run in a cold matrix
dumps its whole report to /tmp/oracle-fail-*.json instead of only its failure strings.
